-- title:
--   The loading velocity is elementary in the target point
-- statement:
--   A parametrized field $f(p)(t,x)$, with $p\in\mathbb R^d$, is elementary when each component of $(t,x,p)\mapsto f(p)(t,x)$ is computed on $\mathbb R^{4+d}$ by a straight-line numeric program. The program is built from rational constants, coordinates, sums, products, negation, guarded inverses, $\exp$, logarithms of rationals and derivatives of $e^{-1/x}$. Let $\eta$ be rational and $p_*=(4,0,0)$. For a target point $p\in\mathbb R^3$, the loading velocity moves a neighbourhood of $p_*$ rigidly along $t\mapsto p_*+S(2t-\frac12)(p-p_*)$, where $S$ is the smooth transition. Then
--   $$
--   p\mapsto u_{\mathrm{load}}(p)\ \text{ is elementary, with parameters }p\in\mathbb R^3 .
--   $$
--
--   When the target point is the rational code of the machine's initial configuration, the loading phase can therefore be computed from that code.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/MathematicalPhysics/NavierStokes/BalancedTransport/ElementaryFields.lean#L160-L179

